---
import CodeBlock from '@/components/code-block.astro'
import PackageManagerTabs from '@/components/PackageManagerTabs.astro'
import Docs from '@/layouts/Docs.astro'

import { Callout, Table } from '@santi020k/lumen-astro'
---

<Docs
  title="Installation"
  description="Step-by-step setup for the CLI, ESLint plugin, VS Code extension, and GitHub Action. Zero config required."
  editPath="apps/docs/src/pages/docs/installation.astro"
>
  <header class="docs-page-header">
    <h1>Installation</h1>
    <p class="docs-page-lead">
      Start with a single scan, then add Astro Doctor to your project, editor, or CI workflow.
    </p>
  </header>
  <h2>CLI only (no config required)</h2>

  <p>Run astro-doctor without installing it at all:</p>

  <PackageManagerTabs
    pnpm="pnpm dlx @santi020k/astro-doctor@latest"
    npm="npx @santi020k/astro-doctor@latest"
    yarn="yarn dlx @santi020k/astro-doctor@latest"
    bun="bunx @santi020k/astro-doctor@latest"
  />

  <p>This scans the current directory and prints a health report with score.</p>

  <h2>Install as a dev dependency</h2>

  <p>Add to your project so it's always available and pinned to a version:</p>

  <PackageManagerTabs
    pnpm="pnpm add -D @santi020k/astro-doctor"
    npm="npm install --save-dev @santi020k/astro-doctor"
    yarn="yarn add -D @santi020k/astro-doctor"
    bun="bun add -D @santi020k/astro-doctor"
  />

  <h2>VS Code extension</h2>

  <p>
    For the best experience in VS Code or Cursor, install the official Astro Doctor extension from the
    <a href="https://marketplace.visualstudio.com/items?itemName=santi020k.vscode-astro-doctor" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Marketplace</a>:
  </p>

  <CodeBlock
    code="ext install santi020k.vscode-astro-doctor"
    language="bash"
    theme="auto"
    variant="block"
    copy
  />

  <p>
    This provides live diagnostics, hovers, quick fixes, and a visual health score sidebar. See
    <a href="/docs/vscode-extension">VS Code Extension</a> for full features.
  </p>

  <h2>ESLint plugin</h2>

  <p>
    For live in-editor diagnostics (VS Code, Neovim, WebStorm, etc.), install the ESLint plugin
    and add it to your flat config:
  </p>

  <PackageManagerTabs
    pnpm="pnpm add -D @santi020k/eslint-plugin-astro-doctor"
    npm="npm install --save-dev @santi020k/eslint-plugin-astro-doctor"
    yarn="yarn add -D @santi020k/eslint-plugin-astro-doctor"
    bun="bun add -D @santi020k/eslint-plugin-astro-doctor"
  />

  <CodeBlock
    code={`// eslint.config.js
import astroDoctorPlugin from '@santi020k/eslint-plugin-astro-doctor'

export default [
  astroDoctorPlugin.configs.recommended,
  // Add your other config here
]`}
    label="eslint.config.js"
    language="javascript"
    theme="auto"
    variant="block"
    copy
  />

  <p>
    The <code>recommended</code> config includes <code>astro-eslint-parser</code> as the parser for
    <code>*.astro</code> files and enables all rules at their default severities.
  </p>

  <h2>Agent skills</h2>

  <p>
    Install the portable Astro Doctor skill so your coding agent can reference the checks and
    their code examples:
  </p>

  <CodeBlock code="astro-doctor install" language="bash" />

  <p>
    This writes <code>skills/astro-doctor.md</code> in your project root. Add
    <code>--agent-hooks</code> for optional Claude Code and Cursor copies. See
    <a href="/docs/agent-skills">Agent skills</a> for destinations and setup options.
  </p>

  <Callout>
    <strong>Tip:</strong> Commit <code>skills/astro-doctor.md</code> to your repository so all
    contributors and their AI tools benefit from the same context.
  </Callout>

  <h2>GitHub Action</h2>

  <p>
    See <a href="/docs/github-action">GitHub Action</a> for full setup instructions. No extra
    installation is required — the action runs astro-doctor internally.
  </p>

  <h2>Works alongside eslint-plugin-astro</h2>

  <p>
    astro-doctor is designed to complement
    <a href="https://ota-meshi.github.io/eslint-plugin-astro/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">eslint-plugin-astro</a>
    (the official community plugin), not replace it. The two cover different ground:
  </p>

  <Table aria-label="ESLint plugin coverage comparison" role="region" tabindex="0">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Coverage area</th>
          <th>eslint-plugin-astro</th>
          <th>astro-doctor</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>Syntax correctness</td>
          <td>Included</td>
          <td>—</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Astro Doctor accessibility rules</td>
          <td>Included</td>
          <td>Focused subset</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Performance (client directives, images, scripts)</td>
          <td>—</td>
          <td>Included</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Security (<code>set:html</code>)</td>
          <td>Included</td>
          <td>Included</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Best practices (<code>class:list</code>, env vars)</td>
          <td>Partial</td>
          <td>Included</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>CLI health score + CI reports</td>
          <td>—</td>
          <td>Included</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>AI agent skills</td>
          <td>—</td>
          <td>Included</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </Table>

  <p>
    If you already use <code>eslint-plugin-astro</code> (e.g. via
    <a href="https://github.com/santi020k/eslint-config-basic" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">eslint-config-basic</a>),
    add astro-doctor on top for performance scanning, health scoring, and AI context.
  </p>
</Docs>
